{"text":"Recent results support text-aware sequential recommendation, but do not establish the exact combination of a from-scratch text encoder, NextItNet, and learned complement/substitute distinctions. [ITS-Rec (2025)](https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/14/9/1748) combines textual item representations with sequential learning, but uses pretrained BERT, a SASRec-derived backbone, and unavailable reviews; its reported gains therefore cannot be assumed under this task’s constraints. [LLM2Rec (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21579) similarly emphasizes adapting language representations to collaborative recommendation signals, rather than learning text embeddings from scratch.

[NextItNet](https://fajieyuan.github.io/papers/nextitnet6.pdf) supplies the relevant backbone: residual dilated causal convolutions trained for next-item prediction. A constraint-compatible hypothesis is to jointly train randomly initialized token embeddings and a small title/description encoder, fuse its output with item-ID embeddings inside one NextItNet, and optimize the entire network through next-item loss. This is a proposed adaptation, not a verified result from the retrieved papers. Separate learned history and candidate projections could allow directional associations without forcing textually similar products to receive similar scores.

The [2024 complementary-recommendation survey](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16135) distinguishes asymmetric complementarity from substitution and discusses their coexistence. Consequently, broad textual similarity should not be treated as a compatibility label. Available histories can supervise observed next-item transitions, but cannot establish that a transition reflects functional complementarity. Test whether jointly learned text improves macro validation HR@10; describe any gains as predictive improvements, without claiming recovered product-purpose or compatibility labels."}
